(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-arterial-occlusive-aneurysm-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arterial Occlusive Disease and Aortic Aneurysm Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"At what age does aortic dissection usually occur outside Marfan syndrome?","choices":["40s to 50s","Over 90","60s to 70s","20s to 30s"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The stem sets Marfan syndrome aside, and outside that exception aortic dissection usually occurs in the 60s and 70s. Males are affected more than females, a history of hypertension is present in most cases, and the most common predisposing factor is degeneration of medial collagen and elastin. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which factor is associated with a decreased risk of abdominal aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Family history","Male sex","Diabetes","Hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diabetes is one of three factors associated with a decreased risk of abdominal aortic aneurysm, with female sex and non-Caucasian race. The reason is unknown; it may reflect diabetic treatment regimens or the increased arterial stiffness that diabetes induces. It is the counterintuitive protective factor to remember. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"By what ratio do asymptomatic patients with significant carotid stenosis outnumber symptomatic ones?","choices":["10:1","4:1","2:1","1:1"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic patients with significant carotid artery stenosis outnumber symptomatic patients by 4:1. Asymptomatic means atherosclerotic narrowing in one or both carotids with no ischemic stroke or transient ischemic attack in the last six months, so most significant stenosis exists before any neurologic event. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Carotid angioplasty with stenting is chosen for which patients?","choices":["Poor surgical candidates","Those with stenosis under 50%","Every asymptomatic patient","Those under 5 years' survival"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carotid angioplasty with stenting is less invasive, most commonly performed through the right or left common femoral artery, so it allows intervention in poor surgical candidates. Carotid endarterectomy, removal of plaque through a surgical incision, remains the gold standard for high-grade stenosis in patients fit for surgery. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of a marginally threatened limb?","choices":["Salvageable only with immediate revascularization","Major tissue loss is inevitable","Under no threat of tissue loss","Salvageable if treated promptly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A marginally threatened limb is salvageable if treated promptly. It has minimal pain in the toes, no sensory loss and no muscle weakness, with audible venous Doppler signals, and there is sufficient time for vascular imaging such as computed tomography angiography. Immediate revascularization is the requirement of the next, immediately threatened, category. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of untreated acute aortic dissection?","choices":["Regression under 2 cm","High mortality","Spontaneous full recovery","Slow, harmless enlargement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute untreated aortic dissection has a high mortality rate. That is why management starts at once: systolic pressure is reduced to 100 to 120 mmHg with a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside, pain is managed, and surgery grafts and replaces the diseased portion of the aorta. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What is the minimum exercise prescription for peripheral artery disease?","choices":["60 minutes, twice weekly","15 minutes, five times weekly","30-45 minutes, three times weekly","10 minutes, once weekly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exercise therapy in peripheral artery disease is a minimum of 30 to 45 minutes three times per week. Consistent moderate exercise improves walking distance, and referral to a supervised rehabilitation program is more successful than a home-based program. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is often the earliest neurologic sign of acute limb ischemia?","choices":["Loss of the ankle reflex","Weakness of toe extension","Numbness over the dorsum of the foot","Paralysis of the calf muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Light-touch sensory fibers are highly susceptible to ischemia, so a sensory deficit over the dorsum of the foot is often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency. That is why sensory testing joins muscle strength testing and the six P's in the examination of acute limb ischemia. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the patient asked to do while the carotid arteries are auscultated?","choices":["Hold the breath","Breathe deeply and quickly","Bear down as if straining","Turn the head to one side"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The carotid examination inspects the pulses in the neck, then asks the patient to hold the breath while each carotid artery is auscultated one at a time, first with the diaphragm and then with the bell, listening for a bruit. Each carotid is then palpated one at a time. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which feature separates peripheral neuropathy from claudication?","choices":["Pain relieved by rest","Pain in the calf muscle","Pain brought on by walking","Pain unaffected by exercise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral neuropathy, common in elderly patients with diabetes or alcoholism, causes a continuous burning sensation in the foot that is unaffected by exercise. Claudication, by contrast, is tied to activity and eased by rest, so pain indifferent to exercise is the separating feature. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which study is best for abdominal aortic aneurysm diagnosis and pre-intervention planning?","choices":["Abdominal ultrasound","Abdominal X-ray","Transesophageal echocardiography","Computed tomography angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography is the best diagnostic and pre-intervention planning study for an abdominal aortic aneurysm, accurately delineating its location, size and extent. Computed tomography in general has an advantage over ultrasound for defining aortic size, visceral artery involvement and extent. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What is peripheral bypass grafting principally used to treat?","choices":["Severe critical limb ischemia","Mild intermittent claudication","Asymptomatic carotid narrowing","Small abdominal aneurysms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bypass grafting is invasive surgery principally used to treat severe critical leg ischemia, bypassing diseased segments with a saphenous vein or synthetic graft. It is highly effective, but its complications include myocardial infarction, stroke, graft infection and peripheral embolization. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which history question best measures claudication?","choices":["Did the pain follow a cold?","How far can you walk before pain?","Does coughing make the pain worse?","Is the pain worse after meals?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Claudication is activity-related muscle pain relieved by rest, usually within 10 minutes, so the key measure is how far the patient can walk before the pain starts. The history also asks where the pain is, whether that distance has changed, and how long the pain takes to go away. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which set is the six P's of arterial assessment?","choices":["Pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis, purpura","Pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis, poikilothermia","Pain, pallor, pulselessness, pruritus, paralysis, poikilothermia","Pain, pallor, petechiae, paresthesia, paralysis, poikilothermia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The six P's of arterial assessment are pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis and poikilothermia (difficulty regulating body temperature). In acute limb ischemia they accompany a cold, cyanotic extremity with absent pulses distal to the occlusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"When is urgent vascular referral needed regardless of aneurysm size?","choices":["Early satiety alone","A new diagnosis of diabetes","A single high blood pressure","Aneurysmal pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Urgent vascular specialist referral is needed if the patient complains of aneurysmal pain, regardless of aneurysm size. Without pain, referral for observation and assessment begins at 4.5 cm, and surgical referral is considered at 5.0 cm or larger. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"How does ischemic rest pain respond to analgesics?","choices":["Poorly, even to narcotics","Completely, to simple analgesics","Only to anti-inflammatory drugs","Well, once the leg is raised"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain of critical limb ischemia is constant, throbbing or burning pain in the forefoot and toes that is not readily controlled by analgesics, even narcotics. That is why the history asks whether pain medications gave relief and how the patient relieves the pain, often by changing position. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does an irreversibly ischemic limb require?","choices":["Urgent bypass grafting","Heparin and observation","Major amputation","Catheter-directed thrombolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An irreversibly ischemic limb has major tissue loss or permanent nerve damage, profound sensory loss, profound weakness with paralysis, and inaudible arterial and venous Doppler signals. Nonviable extremities require major amputation regardless of the therapy instituted, and vascular imaging is generally not immediately needed. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What does the word part \"athero\" in atherosclerosis refer to?","choices":["The clot at a site of rupture","The fatty core of the plaque","The fibrous cap over the plaque","The calcium in the arterial media"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Athero is Greek for gruel or paste and refers to the fatty material in the core of the plaque. Sclerosis, meaning hardness, refers to the stiffening of the arterial wall by the fibrous tissue that forms over the plaque. The plaque itself begins as a fatty streak. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which acute limb ischemia categories leave time for vascular imaging?","choices":["Immediately threatened only","Irreversibly damaged only","All four categories","Viable and marginally threatened"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viable limbs, under no immediate threat of tissue loss, and marginally threatened limbs, salvageable if treated promptly, both have sufficient time for vascular imaging such as computed tomography angiography. Neither has sensory loss or muscle weakness, so a short delay for imaging does not cost the limb. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What causes the pain of a pseudoaneurysm?","choices":["Swelling or nerve compression","Muscle ischemia on walking","A tear through the intima","Retroperitoneal bleeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm presents with pain due to increased pressure from swelling or from nerve compression. It forms when arterial rupture, commonly laceration of the femoral artery by the access needle of a cardiac catheterization, lets blood fill a cavity outside the vessel wall. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Where do diminished or absent pulses appear in peripheral artery disease?","choices":["Below the stenosis","Above the stenosis","Only in the arms","Only at the stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diminished or absent pulses occur below the level of the arterial stenosis or occlusion. That is why pulses are assessed from the brachial and radial down through the aorta, femoral, popliteal, dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial arteries, so tracing them downward shows where flow is lost. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is true of aneurysms in women despite their lower incidence?","choices":["They rupture more often","They never need repair","They grow only in the chest","They rupture less often"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Female sex is associated with a decreased risk of abdominal aortic aneurysm, yet women with aneurysms rupture more often. That pairing is the trap: lower incidence but a more dangerous course once present, and elective repair in women is advised at 5.0 cm or larger, compared with over 5.5 cm in men. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 88, 95"},{"q":"Why does walking around relieve ischemic rest pain, unlike claudication?","choices":["Movement empties the leg veins","Dependency lets gravity aid flow","Muscle work builds new vessels","Walking lowers blood pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain is relieved by hanging the extremity over the edge of the bed or by walking around. The paradox is that, in contrast to claudication, walking around uses the gravity effect of dependence on extremity perfusion, so the foot is better supplied when it hangs down. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which growth rate indicates repair of a thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Over 0.1 cm per year","Over 0.5 cm per year","Over 0.5 cm per month","Over 2 cm per year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Growth of more than 0.5 cm per year is an indication for surgical repair of a thoracic aortic aneurysm, alongside size thresholds of over 5.5 cm for ascending and over 6.0 cm for descending aneurysms. Any symptomatic thoracic aneurysm is also repaired. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which history is common in thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Recent viral illness","Hypothyroidism","Hypertension","Diabetes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A history of hypertension is common in thoracic aortic aneurysm. Most patients are asymptomatic and the aneurysm is an incidental finding on chest radiograph, while symptoms, when present, depend on location: ascending, arch or descending. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"How often is a thoracic aortic aneurysm under 4.5 cm imaged?","choices":["Every 6-12 months","Every month","Every 10 years","Every 3-5 years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An aortic root, ascending or descending aneurysm under 4.5 cm is followed with computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging every 3 to 5 years. At 4.5 to 5.4 cm the interval shortens to every 6 to 12 months, and repair is generally indicated above 5.5 cm ascending or 6.0 cm descending. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"What makes up atheroemboli?","choices":["Clot formed in the left atrium","Air drawn into a vein","Fibrin, platelets and cholesterol","Fat droplets from bone marrow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atheroembolism is multiple small deposits of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris embolizing from proximal atherosclerotic lesions. The debris lodges in small vessels of muscle and skin and may not occlude large vessels, so distal pulses usually remain palpable. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How is an ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm usually repaired?","choices":["Open repair with root replacement","Thrombin injection into the sac","Endovascular repair only","Medical therapy with no repair"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm often requires an open surgical approach with aortic root replacement. A descending one can be repaired open or by endovascular aortic repair, and asymptomatic repair depends on diameter, location and expansion rate, with ascending repair generally above 5.5 cm. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which vasculitis is listed among the causes of aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Medial calcific sclerosis","Leriche syndrome","Arteriolar sclerosis","Takayasu arteritis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vasculitis is a listed cause of aortic aneurysm, with Takayasu arteritis and giant cell arteritis as the examples. The other listed causes are Marfan syndrome, syphilis (infection that destroys the vasa vasorum), trauma and congenital aortic aneurysms. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What does an endarterectomy do?","choices":["Dissolves clot through a catheter","Removes plaque through open surgery","Dilates the artery with a balloon","Reroutes flow around the blockage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endarterectomy is invasive surgery that removes plaque directly from inside an artery via open surgery. It sits with bypass grafting and amputation among the open surgical options, while angioplasty with or without stenting and atherectomy are the endovascular ones. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 58"}]};
